{
  "abstract": "Tao's almost-all Collatz theorem uses a decay estimate $|\\chi_n(\\xi)|\\ll_A n^{-A}$ for the characteristic function $\\chi_n(\\xi)=\\mathbb{E}\\,e(\\xi\\,\\mathrm{Syrac}(\\mathbb{Z}/3^n)/3^n)$ of the Syracuse random variable. We study the true size of $M_n=\\max_{3\\nmid\\xi}|\\chi_n(\\xi)|$. (1) A renewal identity expresses $\\chi_n(2^ju)$ through a cold-walk term and the lower-level values $\\chi_\\ell(u)$, weighted by the first time the walk $s_m$ exceeds $j$. (2) A conditional transfer theorem: if $|\\chi_\\ell(1)|\\le B\\rho^\\ell$ with $\\rho<\\lambda-1\\approx0.585$ ($\\lambda=\\log_23$), then $|\\chi_n(2^j)|\\le Ce^{0.3036\\,j}(\\lambda-1)^n$, which equals $Ce^{-In}$ at $j=\\lambda n$, with $I=\\lambda(\\ln2-H(1/\\lambda))\\approx0.05498$. (3) An $O(n^2)$-operation algorithm evaluates a single coefficient, with absolute truncation control. Float values of $|\\chi_\\ell(1)|$ for $\\ell\\le800$ decay like $e^{-0.565\\ell}$. Ball arithmetic certifies the upper envelope $|\\chi_\\ell(1)|\\le5.6961\\cdot0.58^\\ell$ for $\\ell\\le1000$, which proves the bound of (2) along $\\xi=2^j$ for all $n\\le1000$ with $C=39$ (computer-assisted). (4) We compute $M_n$ for $n\\le18$, and the maximum $M_n^*$ along $\\xi=2^j$ near $j=\\lambda n$ up to $n=2000$. Fitted rates are $0.0552$–$0.0553$. A tilted recursion run to $n=3\\times10^5$ matches a zero-parameter limit formula of cassian_mahler, to $0.1\\%$ at $n\\approx3\\times10^5$, and gives $M^*_n\\asymp n^{-3/2}e^{-In}$ (numerical). Multiplier scans at $n=40,80,150$ support extremality of this family, and certified lower bounds at six levels lie within a factor $1.6\\times10^4$ of the upper bound. We conjecture $\\frac1n\\log M_n\\to-I$. This rate is ten times slower than the rate $\\tfrac12\\ln3$ of a random map that keeps the geometric input weights. Limitations: no bound for all $n$; extremality beyond $n=18$ and relative accuracy of large-$n$ values are supported only numerically; and Fourier decay is not what limits the density exponent in Tao's argument, so these results do not strengthen his theorem.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "v3 adds Remark 3.1, crediting cassian_mahler (r/collatz post 21). (1) The weights of Theorem 1 are exact: |W_l| <= P(tau=n-l+1) = C(j,n-l)2^{-j}, since the partial sums form a Bernoulli(1/2) subset. With the certified chi_l(1), the resulting upper bound is within 22.4-139.9x of the certified lower bounds in Table 2, 62-111x tighter than Corollary 3. (2) A reflection horizon: for j<2n the weights with l>2n-j-1 sum to P(s_n<=j). So Corollary 3 (constant 40) extends to all (n,j) with 2n-j-1<=1000, which includes the extremal window and Table 2 up to n=2395; smaller j at n>1000 stay uncovered. Both identities are checked exactly (new scripts binomial_ub.py and check_reflection_horizon.py). (3) Consistency fix: Remark (iii) now says the hypothesis is certified for l<=1000, as in Section 4.3 and Corollary 3 (it said 800). (4) New §4.7: the polynomial correction. Fits on n<=2000 left beta undetermined; the twisted Pascal recursion of [9] in Cramér-tilted, unwrapped form runs to n=3e5 (wrapped cells validated exact to n=8000). An independent solver for cassian_mahler's Milne limit formula (post 21) gives |Sigma|=309.18, and the finite-n formula matches the lattice to 0.1% and 0.002 rad at n~3e5 (offsets d=-9,-6,-3; at d=0 the hard kill in g* at z<0 must be dropped, which leaves d<=-3 unchanged). So beta=3/2 along the extremal family (NUMERICAL), with the constant predicted; the slow drift is the Gaussian onset of the dominant renewal level l~131. Steps to a theorem are listed. §4.2, §5 (C1+) and contribution 1 are updated; new scripts are in code/collab_c21/.",
